messages:
ashley_cs: Quick heads-up: onboarding run for ScaleQ hit a weird auth/rate pattern. Customer reports initial websocket auth OK, then a 429 on preconnect and a subsequent 401 after a token rotation. They pasted logs.

raj_dev: Can you drop the log snippet + SDK version?

ashley_cs: Using browser SDK v1.4.2. Minimal logs:```
[2026-03-22T08:12:11Z] ws-open ok id=conn-01 auth=200 subproto=redwood-ws
[2026-03-22T08:12:43Z] http 429 GET /v1/clients/preconnect x-ratelimit-remaining=0 retry-after=18
[2026-03-22T08:12:44Z] ws-close id=conn-01 reason=rate_limited
[2026-03-22T08:12:51Z] ws-open id=conn-02 auth=401 token_expired
```

olivia_sre: That pattern screams "preconnect burst". The 429 is from our preconnect endpoint (used by the SDK to negotiate session tokens/limits) — not the WS handshake itself. How many connections did they spawn?

ashley_cs: They said their SPA creates a worker per document; during onboarding they had ~10-15 tabs open which all connect on load.

raj_dev: OK. Actionable guidance: do NOT open a new preconnect per tab. Use a shared connection/pool per user session (max 3-4 concurrent). Also, honor Retry-After header and implement jittered backoff on reconnects. Example pseudo:```js
// simple per-user pool (max 4 concurrent)
const pools = new Map();
async function connectShared(userId){
  if (pools.has(userId)) return pools.get(userId);
  const pool = createPool({max:4});
  pools.set(userId, pool);
  return pool.acquire();
}
// respect Retry-After from 429s
async function handlePreconnect(){
  try { return await fetch('/v1/clients/preconnect'); }
  catch(e){ await wait(jitter(backoff)); }
}
```

mike_support: Also confirm auth header usage. The SDK expects `Authorization: Bearer <token>` during preconnect/handshake. If they rotate tokens frequently, ensure they perform a controlled reconnect sequence instead of letting all tabs reconnect simultaneously. Token rotation should trigger staggered reconnects or a token refresh proxy.

ashley_cs: They also asked what heartbeat interval to use because connections were dropped ~30s after the 429.

olivia_sre: Heartbeat ~30s is OK but add jitter (random ±5s). More importantly: when a connection is closed due to rate limits, clients should back off and limit concurrent reconnect attempts across tabs/workers. Add exponential backoff capped at ~60s and a per-session reconnect semaphore.

raj_dev: I sent them a short mitigation checklist: [ ] consolidate per-user preconnects, [ ] cap pool to 3-4, [ ] honor X-RateLimit/Retry-After headers, [ ] add jittered exponential backoff, [ ] implement graceful token swap with staged reconnects.

ci-bot:policy-scan: preconnect-burst detected for acct=acct123 peak_concurrent=12 recommended=client_pooling

mike_support: Edge case: if they front our API with an HTTP/2 reverse proxy, check for header bloat (cookies/dup headers) which can cause handshake fallbacks and unexpected 401s. Ask for a `curl -I` of the preconnect request so we can inspect headers.

ashley_cs: I'll send the checklist + sample pool snippet and ask for the curl output. Customer will try pooling + jittered backoff and update in ~24h. Will post results here. Thanks all :thumbsup:
